{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.82,"content_digest":"d8e0373c2090ff44740692330bbebad7309d701bf2a40e81f5dfe71193577ef3","created_at":"2026-08-22T14:16:48+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"c0d468464bf77920c20c1a5dadad1739e7fe4e151857921314499e570a8fb894","input_tokens":5932,"latency_ms":35107,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"8224177be7b76f385b0cfde9187aa4915be02e841ba63bb6e9330b5f04a87784","output_tokens":2372,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:dcd422e14fb8bfee654e2072","meta_principle_id":"meta:neuroscience-cognition:integration-segregation","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:neuroscience-cognition:01M0MX8JF16G1Y0XJ270JB41A5","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The principle's claim that hub-containing central regions facilitate communication between spatially separated brain regions is a concrete, simulation-based instance of the integration-segregation meta-principle, which holds that adaptive neural function depends on dynamic coordination across distant regions rather than maximal global connectivity or complete modular isolation. The meta-principle's boundary note that global synchrony can accompany pathology is respected because the principle only reports that coherent global synchrony is preferentially produced by these systems, not that it is universally adaptive.","relation_type":"consistent_with","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T17:07:53+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0N72WTZDNS3VF5QZJB70PZ5"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-found